-- =============================================================================
-- 0054_say_it_dont_cite_it.sql
--
-- GENERATED by scripts/build-unbook.mjs. Do not hand-edit.
--
-- Fifty sentences said things like "the book is careful to say the position was
-- given to him", "the book says to learn these from a teacher", "the book asks
-- for the ten postures of Surya Namaskara here". The reader is performing a
-- rite, not reading a review of an edition. They did not buy the book, do not
-- have it open and cannot check it, so citing it adds a party to the
-- conversation who is not in the room -- and in instruction_en it is worse than
-- noise, because an instruction that reports what somebody else asks for is not
-- an instruction.
--
-- THE VOICE CHANGES, NOT THE CONTENT. "The book says to learn these from a
-- teacher" becomes "These are properly learnt from a teacher": same fact, same
-- authority, stated rather than cited. Nothing is softened and no claim is
-- dropped, because none of these claims were the book's opinion -- they are the
-- tradition's, and the book was reporting them too.
--
-- WHERE PROVENANCE GOES. It already had a home: source_ref carries the edition
-- and page on every row and is never shown to the reader. So the handful of
-- sentences that were pure provenance -- "the Bhavan edition prints it the same
-- way, so two independent editions agree" -- are dropped rather than reworded.
-- They told the reader how confident the transcription is, which is a question
-- about this app and not about their rite.
--
-- Matched as whole sentences and replaced globally, so the copies cannot drift:
-- the Anga Vandanam philosophy is on all three copies of that step and the
-- morning/evening variant note is on four poojas.
-- =============================================================================

begin;

-- Every fragment must still be there. A replace() whose needle is absent is a
-- no-op that reports success -- the quietest failure there is -- and with 38
-- fragments carrying em dashes, curly apostrophes and double hyphens, one of
-- them being a character out is the likeliest thing to go wrong here.
